LEARN.course({id:"jazz-swing",family:"Jazz",name:"Jazz guitar foundations",tagline:"Swing time, ii–V–I harmony and chord-tone lines on a warm archtop.",level:"Intermediate",era:"1930s to today",artists:["Charlie Christian","Freddie Green","Wes Montgomery","Jim Hall","Joe Pass","Kenny Burrell","Pat Metheny"],listen:["Charlie Christian with Benny Goodman – Solo Flight","Count Basie Orchestra – Shiny Stockings","Wes Montgomery – West Coast Blues","Bill Evans & Jim Hall – My Funny Valentine","Joe Pass – Night and Day","Kenny Burrell – Chitlins con Carne"],key:"F major",bpm:140,meter:"4/4",techniques:["jazz-voicings","string-muting","alternate-picking","walking-bass"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Charlie Christian (Charles Henry Christian)",author:"Darren Mueller",site:"Jazz.com Encyclopedia",url:"https://jazz.com/encyclopedia/christian-charlie-charles-henry",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Freddie Green",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freddie_Green",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"Basics of Freddie Green Comping",author:"Tim Berens",site:"freddiegreen.org",url:"https://www.freddiegreen.org/technique/berens.html",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Jazz Guitar Improvisation: Beginning with Guide-Tones",author:"Daniel Andersen",site:"Revista Electrónica de LEEME, no. 26",year:2010,url:"https://pasquin.uv.es/public/journals/35/pdfs/andersen10.pdf",kind:"paper"},{id:"s5",title:"How to Use Guide-Tones to Navigate Chord Changes",author:"Josiah Boornazian",site:"Learn Jazz Standards",year:2017,url:"https://www.learnjazzstandards.com/?p=38425",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Jazz Drop 2 Chord Lesson",author:"Jake Hanlon",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2021,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/jazz/jazz-drop-2-chord-lesson",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s7",title:"Bebop scale",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bebop_scale",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s8",title:"Thirty-two-bar form",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thirty-two_bar_form",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s9",title:"Jazz Blues Chord Progressions",site:"JazzGuitar.be",url:"https://www.jazzguitar.be/blog/jazz-blues-chord-progressions/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s10",title:"A Fresh Approach to the Drumset, Lesson 25: Jazz Swing (excerpt)",author:"Mark Wessels",site:"Vic Firth",url:"https://ae.vicfirth.com/wp-content/uploads/FADrumset-swingin-easy.pdf",kind:"book"},{id:"s11",title:"How To Get A Jazz Guitar Sound",site:"GuitarCommand",url:"https://www.guitarcommand.com/?p=9734",kind:"article"},{id:"s12",title:"Chord Melody Made Easy",site:"JazzGuitar.be",url:"https://www.jazzguitar.be/blog/chord-melody-guitar/",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Jazz Improvisation for Guitar: A Melodic Approach",kind:"book",author:"Garrison Fewell",note:"A Berklee Press melodic approach to jazz guitar soloing. Andersen's guide-tone paper quotes it on connecting arpeggios through guide tones."},{title:"Swing and Big Band Guitar: Four-to-the-Bar Comping in the Style of Freddie Green",kind:"book",author:"Charlton Johnson",url:"https://www.ejazzlines.com/swing-and-big-band-guitar-charlton-johnson-book-online-audio",note:"Rhythm-guitar voicings, time, groove and chart reading for swing and big band, with audio examples."},{title:"Basic Jazz Rhythm Guitar",kind:"video",author:"Mel Bay",url:"https://www.melbay.com/Products/20365MEB/basic-jazz-rhythm-guitar.aspx",note:"A short video course on four-to-the-bar strumming, chord voicings and voice leading."},{title:"Chord Melody with Peter Einhorn",kind:"course",author:"Peter Einhorn",url:"https://truefire.com/c2133",note:"A video course devoted to chord-melody playing, the natural next step after the sketch in this course."},{title:"Charlie Christian – The Genius of the Electric Guitar",kind:"album",note:"His recordings with Benny Goodman, where the single-note electric guitar solo took shape."},{title:"Count Basie – April in Paris",kind:"album",note:"Freddie Green's four-to-the-bar time under a full big band. Listen for the guitar you feel more than hear."},{title:"Bill Evans & Jim Hall – Undercurrent",kind:"album",note:"Guitar and piano duets: a study in comping, space and voice leading."},{title:"Joe Pass – Virtuoso",kind:"album",note:"Solo guitar standards that mix bass lines, chords and single-note lines."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"The sound of swing guitar",body:"Jazz guitar took shape in the swing bands of the 1930s. There the guitar was mostly a rhythm instrument: Freddie Green strummed chords in Count Basie's orchestra from 1937 until 1987 and rarely took a solo.\n\nCharlie Christian opened the other door. After John Hammond brought him to Benny Goodman in 1939, he played amplified single-note solos, and bandmate Jerry Jerome recalled that Goodman had never heard a guitar player who could sound like a tenor saxophone.\n\nUnder both roles sits **swing feel**. Drum method books describe it as a triplet feel built on the ride cymbal, with eighth notes written straight and played long-short.\n\nIn this course you build a medium-swing tune in F: a 32-bar AABA head with four-to-the-bar comping, a chord-tone solo and a chord-melody ending.",cites:["s1","s2","s10"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Before he owned an electric guitar, Christian played into a microphone held between his knees. He went on to play a Gibson ES-150 electric guitar and recorded with Goodman into 1941.",cites:["s1"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"ii–V–I, shells and drop 2",body:"The cadence to learn first is **ii–V–I**. In F that is Gm7, C7, Fmaj7. In a minor key it becomes a half-diminished ii, a dominant V and a minor i, and one harmonic minor scale (with an added passing note) can cover all three chords.\n\nStart comping with three-note **shells**: root, 3rd and 7th. The 3rd and 7th are what separate major 7, minor 7 and dominant 7 chords, so the 5th can go. Andersen's guide-tone method starts from exactly these shapes, and Freddie Green-style players keep them low, mostly on strings 6, 4 and 3.\n\n**Drop-2** voicings add a fourth note. Take a close-position chord such as C E G B, move the second note from the top down an octave, and you get G C E B. Each drop-2 shape sits on four neighboring strings, and many chords used by Wes Montgomery and Joe Pass come from them.",cites:["s4","s5","s3","s6","s7"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Major ii–V–I in F",chords:"ii7 V7 Imaj7 -",note:"Gm7, C7, Fmaj7. Listen for the 7th of each chord falling a half step to the 3rd of the next.",cites:["s5"]},{type:"progression",name:"Minor ii–V–i in D minor",key:"D minor",chords:"iiø7 V7b9 i6 -",note:"Eø7, A7b9, Dm6. The flat 9 on A7 is the Bb of D harmonic minor.",cites:["s7"]},{type:"progression",name:"I–VI–ii–V turnaround",chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7",note:"Fmaj7, D7, Gm7, C7: the loop the comping etude uses and the opening of every A section in the song map."},{type:"shapes",title:"Shell voicings (root, 3rd, 7th)",shapes:[{name:"Gm7",frets:"3 x 3 3 x x"},{name:"C7",frets:"x 3 2 3 x x"},{name:"Fmaj7",frets:"1 x 2 2 x x"},{name:"D7",frets:"x 5 4 5 x x"}],note:"Root on string 6 or 5, 3rd and 7th on strings 4 and 3. These four shapes cover the whole turnaround in the first five frets.",cites:["s3","s4"]},{type:"shapes",title:"Drop-2 voicings, major and minor",shapes:[{name:"Gm7",frets:"x x 5 7 6 6"},{name:"C7",frets:"x 3 5 3 5 x"},{name:"Fmaj7",frets:"x x 3 5 5 5"},{name:"Eø7",frets:"x 7 8 7 8 x"},{name:"A7b9",frets:"x x 7 6 8 6"},{name:"Dm6",frets:"x x 7 7 6 7"}],note:"The first three are the major ii–V–I, the last three the minor ii–V–i. The top note of each shape is the one you can use as a melody note.",cites:["s6"]},{type:"technique",id:"jazz-voicings",note:"Shells for big-band style comping, drop-2 shapes for small groups and chord melody."}]},{id:"comping",title:"Four to the bar",body:"Swing rhythm guitar means a strum on every beat, four to the bar. Tim Berens, who teaches the style, puts time before voicings. He hears a longer sound on beats 1 and 3, a shorter one on 2 and 4, and a slight accent on 2 and 4, made by gripping the pick a little firmer with the same motion.\n\nShorten the notes with your fretting hand: ease the pressure right after each strum. Freddie Green fingered chords across four strings, sounded only some of them and muted the others with his left hand. Keep the volume just under the drums and keep the low end thin so the bass stays clear.\n\nThe drummer's part fits around you. The ride plays the triplet-based pattern, the hi-hat pedal closes on 2 and 4, and the bass drum can feather soft quarter notes that are felt more than heard. Your long strums lock with the ride, your short ones with the hi-hat.",cites:["s3","s2","s10"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"jazz-swing-ride",name:"Swing ride, hi-hat on 2 and 4",role:"Chorus",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{rd:"x..X.xx..X.x",hh:"...x.....x..",kk:"g..g..g..g.."}},note:"Ride on 1, 2, the last triplet of 2, 3, 4 and the last triplet of 4. Hi-hat pedal on 2 and 4, feathered kick on every beat.",cites:["s10"]},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"jazz-swing-hat",name:"Hi-hat swing for the head",role:"Verse",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{oh:"x.....x.....",hh:"...x.x...x.x",kk:"g..g..g..g.."}},note:"An open hi-hat sizzle on 1 and 3 that closes on 2 and 4: a lighter feel for the head before the band moves to the ride.",cites:["s10"]},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-swing-four-to-bar",title:"Four to the bar on ii–V–I–VI",instr:"guitar",bpm:140,grid:3,tone:"jazz-swing-rhythm-tone",notes:[[0,6,3,2,"D"],[0,4,3,2,"D"],[0,3,3,2,"D"],[3,6,3,1,"D,acc"],[3,4,3,1,"D,acc"],[3,3,3,1,"D,acc"],[6,6,3,2,"D"],[6,4,3,2,"D"],[6,3,3,2,"D"],[9,6,3,1,"D,acc"],[9,4,3,1,"D,acc"],[9,3,3,1,"D,acc"],[12,5,3,2,"D"],[12,4,2,2,"D"],[12,3,3,2,"D"],[15,5,3,1,"D,acc"],[15,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[15,3,3,1,"D,acc"],[18,5,3,2,"D"],[18,4,2,2,"D"],[18,3,3,2,"D"],[21,5,3,1,"D,acc"],[21,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[21,3,3,1,"D,acc"],[24,6,1,2,"D"],[24,4,2,2,"D"],[24,3,2,2,"D"],[27,6,1,1,"D,acc"],[27,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[27,3,2,1,"D,acc"],[30,6,1,2,"D"],[30,4,2,2,"D"],[30,3,2,2,"D"],[33,6,1,1,"D,acc"],[33,4,2,1,"D,acc"],[33,3,2,1,"D,acc"],[36,5,5,2,"D"],[36,4,4,2,"D"],[36,3,5,2,"D"],[39,5,5,1,"D,acc"],[39,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[39,3,5,1,"D,acc"],[42,5,5,2,"D"],[42,4,4,2,"D"],[42,3,5,2,"D"],[45,5,5,1,"D,acc"],[45,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[45,3,5,1,"D,acc"]],note:"Gm7, C7, Fmaj7, D7 as shells. Let beats 1 and 3 ring a little, choke 2 and 4 short with a light accent, all downstrokes.",cites:["s3"]},{type:"technique",id:"string-muting",note:"Release the fretting hand after each strum and let spare fingers rest on the strings you do not want to hear."}]},{id:"lines",title:"Chord tones, guide tones and chord melody",body:"Before you reach for scales, learn to hear the chords in your lines. Daniel Andersen's method for beginning jazz guitarists starts from the comping shapes, pulls out their 3rds and 7ths as single-note **guide tones**, then grows them into full arpeggios. The guide tones move by half step or stay put, because the 7th of one chord tends to fall to the 3rd of the next.\n\nWork in three passes over a ii–V–I:\n\n- play only the 3rds and 7ths, one per bar\n- land a 3rd or 7th on beats 1 and 3 and connect them with chord tones and approach notes\n- swing eighth notes through the arpeggios and keep the targets on the beat\n\n**Chord melody** puts the tune on top. Learn the melody on the top two strings first, then fit chord shapes under its important notes. Drop-2 shapes help here: Jake Hanlon's lesson uses them to harmonize a major scale, one chord under each melody note.",cites:["s4","s5","s12","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"jazz-swing-guide-tones",title:"Guide-tone line over ii–V–I in F",instr:"guitar",bpm:140,grid:3,tone:"jazz-swing-archtop",notes:[[0,4,8,2,"D"],[2,3,5,1,"U"],[3,3,7,2,"D"],[5,2,5,1,"U"],[6,2,6,2,"D"],[8,1,5,1,"U"],[9,2,8,2,"D"],[11,2,6,1,"U"],[12,2,5,2,"D"],[14,2,8,1,"U"],[15,1,6,2,"D"],[17,1,5,1,"U"],[18,2,8,2,"D"],[20,2,5,1,"U"],[21,3,5,2,"D"],[23,4,8,1,"U"],[24,4,7,2,"D"],[26,3,5,1,"U"],[27,2,5,2,"D"],[29,2,8,1,"U"],[30,1,5,2,"D"],[32,2,8,1,"U"],[33,2,5,2,"D"],[35,3,5,1,"U"],[36,4,7,9,"D,v"]],note:"Gm7 starts on Bb, its 3rd. Its 7th, F, falls to E (3rd of C7), and C7's 7th, Bb, falls to A (3rd of Fmaj7). Chord tones sit on every beat.",cites:["s4","s5"]},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-swing-minor-line",title:"Minor ii–V–i line in D minor",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:3,tone:"jazz-swing-archtop",notes:[[0,5,7,2,"D"],[2,4,5,1,"U"],[3,4,8,2,"D"],[5,3,7,1,"U"],[6,2,5,2,"D"],[8,2,8,1,"U"],[9,2,6,2,"D"],[11,3,7,1,"U"],[12,3,6,2,"D"],[14,2,5,1,"U"],[15,2,8,2,"D"],[17,1,6,1,"U"],[18,1,5,2,"D"],[20,2,8,1,"U"],[21,2,5,2,"D"],[23,3,6,1,"U"],[24,3,7,2,"D"],[26,2,6,1,"U"],[27,1,5,2,"D"],[29,1,7,1,"U"],[30,1,5,2,"D"],[32,2,6,1,"U"],[33,3,7,2,"D"],[35,3,4,1,"U"],[36,4,7,3,"D"],[39,3,7,9,"D,v"]],note:"An Eø7 arpeggio, then D (7th of Eø7) falls to C# (3rd of A7). The Bb over A7 is the flat 9, and C# rises to D on the Dm6.",cites:["s5","s7"]},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-swing-chord-melody",title:"Chord-melody sketch over ii–V–I",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:3,ring:!0,tone:"jazz-swing-archtop",notes:[[0,4,5,6],[0,3,7,6],[0,2,6,6],[0,1,6,6],[6,1,5,3],[9,1,3,3],[12,5,3,6],[12,4,5,6],[12,3,3,6],[12,2,5,6],[18,1,3,3],[21,1,6,3],[24,4,3,12],[24,3,5,12],[24,2,5,12],[24,1,5,12,"v"]],note:"An original melody, Bb A G, E G Bb, A, with a drop-2 chord under each long note: Gm7, C7, then Fmaj7.",cites:["s12","s6"]},{type:"technique",id:"alternate-picking",note:"The lines here are marked down on the beat and up on the offbeat, which keeps the long-short swing pattern in your pick hand."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Archtop warmth",body:"The traditional jazz guitar is a hollow archtop such as a Gibson ES-175 or L-5, played on the neck pickup with the tone control partly or fully down. Many players string it with **flatwounds**, traditionally 12s or 13s, which sound darker and smoother than roundwounds, and pick with a small, thick pick. Wes Montgomery used his thumb.\n\nThe amp stays clean. Valve combos such as the Fender Deluxe Reverb and Twin Reverb are common, and so are solid-state amps like the Roland JC-120 and the Polytone Brute, which George Benson used. Effects stay minimal; a volume pedal is the most useful extra.\n\nFor big-band rhythm the target changes. Freddie Green played a Stromberg archtop, and Tim Berens plays an L-5 with a cut in the amp's midrange, roughly 330 Hz to 1 kHz, so the guitar sits under the band.",cites:["s11","s2","s3"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"jazz-swing-archtop",name:"Archtop on the neck pickup",kind:"electric",guitar:"Hollowbody archtop (Gibson ES-175 or L-5 type)",pickups:"Neck humbucker",strings:"Flatwound 12s or 13s",amp:"Clean combo with headroom (Fender Twin Reverb or Polytone type)",settings:{gain:2,bass:5,mid:6,treble:3,presence:2,reverb:2},pedals:["volume"],demo:"jazz-swing-guide-tones",tips:["Use the neck pickup and turn the guitar's tone control partly or fully down.","Flatwounds, traditionally 12s or 13s, give a darker, smoother sound than roundwounds.","A small, thick pick such as a Jazz III gives control and a woody attack. Wes Montgomery used his thumb instead.","Stay on the clean channel. A volume pedal helps you balance comping and soloing levels.","Season the reverb to taste: Grant Green used little or none, Pat Metheny rolls off the highs and uses a lot."],cites:["s11"],note:"Turn the treble up and the gain past 4 to hear how quickly the archtop sound turns into a rock tone."},{type:"tone",id:"jazz-swing-rhythm-tone",name:"Big-band rhythm archtop",kind:"acoustic",guitar:"Acoustic archtop (Stromberg or L-5 type)",pickups:"None, or a floating pickup kept low in the mix",amp:"Unamplified, or a clean amp with a midrange notch",settings:{gain:0,bass:4,mid:3,treble:5,reverb:1},pedals:[],demo:"jazz-swing-four-to-bar",tips:["Freddie Green's signature instrument was a Stromberg archtop.","Keep the level just below the drums so the guitar works as pitched percussion.","Tim Berens notches the amp around 330 Hz to 1 kHz so the guitar does not cloud the bass.","Mostly three-note voicings on strings 6, 4 and 3; skip barre chords and big extensions unless the chart asks."],cites:["s2","s3"],note:"A rhythm tone is meant to blend. Play the demo with the band in mind, not on its own."}]},{id:"form",title:"32-bar AABA and the jazz blues",body:"Much of the standard repertoire uses the **32-bar AABA** form of Tin Pan Alley and Broadway songwriters such as Gershwin, Porter and Kern: three 8-bar A sections that share a melody and a contrasting 8-bar bridge. Jazz players call one pass through the form a chorus and solo for a number of choruses over the same changes.\n\nThe other form you need is the **12-bar blues** with jazz changes. Teaching versions run from the 1930s changes through Count Basie and bebop variants to Charlie Parker's Bird blues, usually written in F. The version below adds a IV chord in bar 2, a diminished passing chord in bar 6 and ii–V motion at the end.\n\nThe song map is an original AABA in F. The head uses the hi-hat groove and the bridge and solos move to the ride, the same move drum method books teach: hi-hat feel first, then the ride.",cites:["s8","s9","s10"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"12-bar jazz blues in F",chords:"I7 IV7 I7 v7 IV7 #iv°7 I7 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 V7",note:"F7 Bb7 F7 Cm7, Bb7 B°7 F7 D7, Gm7 C7 F7 C7. With one chord per bar, bar 4 stands in for Cm7 to F7 and bar 12 for a full turnaround.",cites:["s9"]},{type:"form",name:"32-bar AABA tune in F",sections:[{type:"intro",name:"Intro turnaround",bars:4,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7",groove:"jazz-swing-hat",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A1",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 iii7 VI7 ii7 V7",groove:"jazz-swing-hat",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A2",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 -",groove:"jazz-swing-hat",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"bridge",name:"B (bridge)",bars:8,chords:"III7 - VI7 - II7 - V7 -",groove:"jazz-swing-ride",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",name:"A3",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 -",groove:"jazz-swing-hat",fill:""},{type:"solo",name:"Solo chorus (AABA)",bars:32,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 iii7 VI7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 - III7 - VI7 - II7 - V7 - Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 -",groove:"jazz-swing-ride",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"outro",name:"Head out and chord-melody tag",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 VI7 ii7 V7 ii7 V7 Imaj7 -",groove:"jazz-swing-hat",fill:""}],note:"The bridge runs A7, D7, G7, C7, each dominant falling a fifth to the next until C7 leads home to F.",cites:["s8"]},{type:"technique",id:"walking-bass",note:"Under the solos the bass walks quarter notes, and the feathered kick and your four-to-the-bar strum sit on the same pulse.",cites:["s10"]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Build your own tune on the song map. Write an 8-bar A melody over the A changes with a chord tone on most strong beats, repeat it, then write a bridge that follows the dominant chain and leads back to the last A.\n\nPlay the head with shell voicings and the hi-hat groove, then take one solo chorus that aims for 3rds and 7ths at every chord change. End with a chord-melody version of the last A: melody on top, drop-2 shapes under the long notes.\n\nRecord yourself and listen for two things. Can you hear the guide tones move when the chords change? Do your strums sit with the ride cymbal and the hi-hat?",cites:["s8","s4","s5","s3","s12"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Comp four to the bar through the ii–V–I–VI loop at 140 bpm, short on 2 and 4.","Play shells and drop-2 shapes for Gm7, C7 and Fmaj7 without looking.","Play a guide-tone line (3rds and 7ths only) through your A section.","Solo one chorus with a chord tone on beats 1 and 3 of every bar.","Arrange the last A as chord melody with the tune on top.","Set the amp clean, neck pickup on, tone rolled back."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"bebop",family:"Jazz",name:"Bebop and hard bop guitar",tagline:"Fast eighth-note lines that hit the changes, built from enclosures, bebop scales and arpeggios.",level:"Advanced",era:"Early 1940s to today",artists:["Charlie Parker","Dizzy Gillespie","Charlie Christian","Barney Kessel","Joe Pass","Wes Montgomery","Kenny Burrell","Grant Green"],listen:["Charlie Parker – Ko-Ko","Wes Montgomery – Four on Six","Kenny Burrell – Chitlins con Carne","Grant Green – Grant's First Stand (album)","Miles Davis Quintet – Oleo"],key:"Bb major",bpm:200,meter:"4/4",techniques:["alternate-picking","economy-picking","string-muting","jazz-voicings"],sources:[{id:"b1",title:"Minton's Playhouse",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minton%27s_Playhouse",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b2",title:"Bebop",site:"EBSCO Research Starters",url:"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/music/bebop/",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b3",title:"Charlie Christian (Charles Henry Christian)",author:"Darren Mueller",site:"Jazz.com Encyclopedia",url:"https://jazz.com/encyclopedia/christian-charlie-charles-henry",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b4",title:"Rhythm changes",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm_changes",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b5",title:"Bebop scale",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bebop_scale",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b6",title:"Chromatic Approach Notes",site:"Fundamental Changes",url:"https://fundamental-changes.com/?p=5399",kind:"lesson"},{id:"b7",title:"Tritone Substitution: The Ultimate Guide",author:"Steve Nixon",site:"Free Jazz Lessons",year:2020,url:"https://www.freejazzlessons.com/tritone-chord-substitution/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"b8",title:"Wes Montgomery Octaves",author:"Jim Bastian",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2008,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/wes-montgomery-octaves",kind:"lesson"},{id:"b9",title:"Masterpiece: Wes Montgomery's West Coast Blues",author:"Jeff Jacobson",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2026,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/masterpiece-wes-montgomerys-west-coast-blues",kind:"article"},{id:"b10",title:"Styles & Forms: Hard Bop",site:"Flame Tree",url:"https://www.flametreepro.com/styles-and-forms-hard-bop-jazz.html",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"b11",title:"A Fresh Approach to the Drumset, Lesson 25: Jazz Swing (excerpt)",author:"Mark Wessels",site:"Vic Firth",url:"https://ae.vicfirth.com/wp-content/uploads/FADrumset-swingin-easy.pdf",kind:"book"},{id:"b12",title:"How To Get A Jazz Guitar Sound",site:"GuitarCommand",url:"https://www.guitarcommand.com/?p=9734",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"Bebop Vocabulary Fundamentals for Jazz Guitar, Vol. 1",kind:"course",author:"Denis Chang",url:"https://www.soundslice.com/store/bebop-vocabulary-fundamentals-for-jazz-guitar-vol1/",note:"Builds bebop lines from enclosures, arpeggio fragments and the bebop scale, from the viewpoint of Christian, Kessel and Pass."},{title:"Charlie Parker Omnibook",kind:"book",note:"Parker's solos transcribed. Learn short phrases by ear first, then check them on the page."},{title:"The Barry Harris Harmonic Method for Guitar",kind:"book",author:"Alan Kingstone",note:"Puts Barry Harris's bebop-scale and sixth-diminished ideas on the fretboard."},{title:"How to Play Bebop",kind:"book",author:"David Baker",note:"From the teacher who named the bebop scales: scale drills, patterns and tunes."},{title:"Jazz Blues Soloing for Guitar",kind:"book",author:"Joseph Alexander",note:"The Fundamental Changes book the chromatic approach lesson comes from, worked through a jazz blues."},{title:"Wes Montgomery – The Incredible Jazz Guitar of Wes Montgomery",kind:"album",note:"Octaves, thumb-picked lines and chord solos, including West Coast Blues and Four on Six."},{title:"Kenny Burrell – Midnight Blue",kind:"album",note:"Blues-rooted hard bop guitar with clear, direct phrasing."},{title:"Grant Green – Grant's First Stand",kind:"album",note:"Horn-like single-note lines over an organ trio."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"Where bebop came from",body:"Bebop grew out of after-hours jam sessions in Harlem in the early 1940s. At **Minton's Playhouse** a house band with Thelonious Monk on piano and Kenny Clarke on drums hosted guests such as Dizzy Gillespie and, from 1942, Charlie Parker. To keep weaker players off the stand, Gillespie called standards in hard keys, and fast tempos did the same job.\n\nA guitarist was part of that scene. Charlie Christian played at Minton's most nights, and Parker said he tried to get Christian's sound on the saxophone. Bebop drew on the full chromatic scale and on substituted chords, and players wrote new melodies over existing progressions: Parker's 'Ko-Ko' is built on the changes of 'Cherokee'.\n\nIn the 1950s **hard bop**, associated with Horace Silver and Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers, brought in more blues and gospel phrasing and more original compositions. This course covers the line-playing vocabulary both styles share, applied to the guitar.",cites:["b1","b2","b3","b10"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"In May 1941 Jerry Newman recorded Christian at an after-hours session at Minton's. The historian Gunther Schuller heard in those recordings the start of a new language in jazz.",cites:["b1","b3"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Rhythm changes and the tritone sub",body:"Next to the blues, bebop's staple progression is **rhythm changes**: the chords of Gershwin's 'I Got Rhythm', usually in Bb, in a 32-bar AABA form. The A sections cycle I–vi–ii–V, and the bridge is a chain of dominant 7ths, D7, G7, C7, F7, two bars each.\n\nNew tunes on these changes, such as 'Anthropology' and 'Oleo', gave players a shared jam-session vehicle and a melody they could copyright. Bebop players also layered in ii–V chains and other substitutions, which made the changes harder for less experienced players to join.\n\nThe best-known substitution is the **tritone sub**: replace a V7 with the dominant a tritone away. In Bb, F7 becomes B7 (spelled Cb7) and resolves down a half step into the tonic. The two chords share notes, so a soloist can hint at one while the band plays the other.",cites:["b4","b7"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Rhythm changes A section (one chord per bar)",chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 V7",note:"Bb6, G7, Cm7, F7, Bb7, Eb6, Bb6, F7. In the tune these chords move two to a bar; here each bar holds one so the song model can carry it.",cites:["b4"]},{type:"progression",name:"Rhythm changes bridge",chords:"III7 - VI7 - II7 - V7 -",note:"D7, G7, C7, F7, two bars each, moving round the circle of fourths back to Bb.",cites:["b4"]},{type:"progression",name:"ii–V–I with a tritone sub",chords:"ii7 bII7 Imaj7 -",note:"Cm7, B7, Bbmaj7. The bass walks down C, B, Bb.",cites:["b7"]},{type:"progression",name:"Minor ii–V–i in G minor",key:"G minor",chords:"iiø7 V7b9 i6 -",note:"Aø7, D7b9, Gm6. One bebop harmonic minor scale can cover all three chords.",cites:["b5"]},{type:"technique",id:"jazz-voicings",note:"Comp rhythm changes with shells and drop-2 shapes, moving one voice at a time as the chords cycle."}]},{id:"time",title:"Bebop time feel",body:"In bebop the drummer keeps the pulse on the ride cymbal and uses the snare and bass drum to accent and comment, and the smaller rhythm sections push the soloist more actively. Kenny Clarke and Max Roach both shaped this way of playing.\n\nThe first groove below keeps the swing ride, the hi-hat pedal on 2 and 4 and a feathered kick, then adds quiet snare comping on the last triplet of a beat and a kick accent at the end of the bar. The second groove moves the time to the hi-hat for the head.\n\nYour eighth notes live on the same triplet grid. Jazz rhythms are usually written as straight eighths and played long-short, the first note taking two thirds of the beat. Practice each line here slowly with that grid in your head before you raise the tempo.",cites:["b10","b2","b11"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"bebop-ride",name:"Bebop ride with snare comping",role:"Chorus",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{rd:"x..X.xx..X.x",hh:"...x.....x..",sn:".....g..g...",kk:"g..g..g..g.x"}},note:"Ride and hi-hat keep the time. The ghosted snare and the kick on the last triplet of beat 4 are the drummer's comments.",cites:["b11","b2"]},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"bebop-head-hat",name:"Hi-hat time for the head",role:"Verse",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{oh:"x.....x.....",hh:"...x.x...x.x",sn:"..g.....g...",kk:"g..g..g..g.."}},note:"Sizzling hi-hat on 1 and 3, closed on 2 and 4, for stating the melody before the solos open up on the ride.",cites:["b11"]},{type:"technique",id:"alternate-picking",note:"The etudes mark down on the beat and up on the offbeat. Keep the motion small so it holds up at speed."}]},{id:"lines",title:"Enclosures, bebop scales and arpeggios",body:"Bebop lines put chord tones on strong beats. The **dominant bebop scale** adds a chromatic passing note between the b7 and the root of Mixolydian. Start it on a chord tone on a downbeat and the root, 3rd, 5th and b7 stay on the beats. David Baker named these bebop scales because Christian, Parker, Bud Powell and Gillespie used them so often.\n\nChromatic notes also lead into targets. If you are a whole step from the next chord tone, slip a chromatic passing note onto the offbeat between them. If you are a half step away, surround the target: a note above, a note below, then the target on the beat. That is an **enclosure**, and it can use chromatic or scale notes.\n\nThe three etudes below are original. Each one outlines the chords with arpeggios, keeps chord tones on the beats and uses an enclosure to land on the 3rd of the next chord.",cites:["b5","b6"],blocks:[{type:"scale",root:"F",scale:"bebopDom",view:"box",note:"F Mixolydian plus E natural. Descend from F, Eb or C on a downbeat over F7 and the chord tones fall on the beats.",cites:["b5"]},{type:"tab",id:"bebop-enclosure-251",title:"Enclosure etude: ii–V–I in Bb",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:3,tone:"bebop-archtop",notes:[[0,1,6,2,"D"],[2,2,8,1,"U"],[3,3,8,2,"D"],[5,3,5,1,"U"],[6,3,7,2,"D"],[8,3,5,1,"U"],[9,4,8,2,"D"],[11,4,6,1,"U"],[12,4,7,2,"D"],[14,3,5,1,"U"],[15,3,8,2,"D"],[17,2,8,1,"U"],[18,2,6,2,"D"],[20,2,5,1,"U"],[21,3,8,2,"D"],[23,3,6,1,"U"],[24,3,7,2,"D"],[26,2,6,1,"U"],[27,1,5,2,"D"],[29,1,8,1,"U"],[30,1,6,2,"D"],[32,1,5,1,"U"],[33,2,8,2,"D"],[35,2,6,1,"U"],[36,3,7,9,"D,v"]],note:"Cm7, F7, Bbmaj7. Bb and G# enclose A, the 3rd of F7. Over F7 the bebop scale runs F, E, Eb, then Eb and C# enclose D, the 3rd of Bbmaj7.",cites:["b5","b6"]},{type:"tab",id:"bebop-tritone-sub",title:"Tritone sub etude: Cm7, B7, Bbmaj7",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:3,tone:"bebop-archtop",notes:[[0,3,5,2,"D"],[2,3,8,1,"U"],[3,2,8,2,"D"],[5,1,6,1,"U"],[6,1,5,2,"D"],[8,2,8,1,"U"],[9,3,8,2,"D"],[11,4,8,1,"U"],[12,4,7,2,"D"],[14,4,9,1,"U"],[15,3,8,2,"D"],[17,2,7,1,"U"],[18,1,5,2,"D"],[20,2,7,1,"U"],[21,3,8,2,"D"],[23,3,6,1,"U"],[24,3,7,2,"D"],[26,2,6,1,"U"],[27,1,5,9,"D,v"]],note:"Over B7 the line spells A, B, D#, F#. Bb falls to A, and D# with C# encloses D, the 3rd of Bbmaj7.",cites:["b6","b7"]},{type:"tab",id:"bebop-rhythm-bridge",title:"Rhythm changes bridge etude",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:3,tone:"bebop-archtop",notes:[[0,2,7,2,"D"],[2,1,5,1,"U"],[3,1,8,2,"D"],[5,1,5,1,"U"],[6,2,7,2,"D"],[8,3,7,1,"U"],[9,3,5,2,"D"],[11,4,8,1,"U"],[12,4,9,2,"D"],[14,3,7,1,"U"],[15,2,6,2,"D"],[17,2,8,1,"U"],[18,1,7,2,"D"],[20,1,5,1,"U"],[21,2,6,2,"D"],[23,3,8,1,"U"],[24,2,5,2,"D"],[26,2,8,1,"U"],[27,1,6,2,"D"],[29,2,8,1,"U"],[30,2,5,2,"D"],[32,3,5,1,"U"],[33,4,8,2,"D"],[35,4,6,1,"U"],[36,4,7,2,"D"],[38,3,5,1,"U"],[39,3,8,2,"D"],[41,2,6,1,"U"],[42,1,5,2,"D"],[44,2,8,1,"U"],[45,2,6,2,"D"],[47,3,8,1,"U"],[48,3,7,6,"D,v"]],note:"D7, G7, C7, F7, one bar each. Every 7th falls to the next chord's 3rd through an enclosure, and the last Eb falls to D as the final A section starts.",cites:["b4","b6"]},{type:"technique",id:"economy-picking",note:"Arpeggios that change string on every note are where economy strokes save motion. Try the etudes both ways and keep what stays even."}]},{id:"octaves",title:"Wes-style octaves",body:"Wes Montgomery made **octaves** a trademark and could play whole melodies in them. He plucked with his thumb, which gives a smooth, warm attack. An octave sits on two strings with one string between them, and that middle string stays muted.\n\nFret the lower note with your first finger and the upper note with your little finger. The shape works on string pairs 6 and 4, 5 and 3, 4 and 2, and 3 and 1. The upper note is two frets higher on the two lower pairs and three frets higher on the pairs that involve the B string. For eighth-note lines most players use all downstrokes, with a pick or the thumb.\n\nA blues is a good place to start. Jim Bastian's Premier Guitar lesson runs octaves through a 12-bar blues to learn the positions along with the blues vocabulary Wes relied on.",cites:["b8","b9"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"bebop-wes-octaves",title:"Octave blues phrase in Bb",instr:"guitar",bpm:150,grid:3,tone:"bebop-thumb",notes:[[0,4,3,2,"D"],[0,2,6,2,"D"],[2,4,6,1,"D"],[2,2,9,1,"D"],[3,4,7,2,"D,s"],[3,2,10,2,"D,s"],[5,4,8,4,"D"],[5,2,11,4,"D"],[9,3,5,2,"D"],[9,1,8,2,"D"],[11,3,7,1,"D"],[11,1,10,1,"D"],[12,3,5,2,"D"],[12,1,8,2,"D"],[14,4,8,4,"D"],[14,2,11,4,"D"],[18,4,5,2,"D"],[18,2,8,2,"D"],[20,4,3,4,"D,v"],[20,2,6,4,"D,v"]],note:"F, Ab sliding to A, Bb, then C, D, C, Bb, G, F, all in octaves on strings 4 and 2, then 3 and 1. Mute the string in between and use downstrokes throughout.",cites:["b8","b9"]},{type:"technique",id:"string-muting",note:"In octaves the fretting hand damps the string between the two notes, so a thumb or pick stroke across three strings still sounds clean."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Bebop and hard bop tone",body:"Bebop guitar tone comes from the same family as swing: a hollow archtop or semi-hollow guitar on the neck pickup, flatwound strings and a clean amp. Wes Montgomery is linked with a Gibson L-5, and Grant Green with an ES-335, a midrange boost and little or no reverb.\n\nFor fast lines a small, thick pick such as a Jazz III or a heavy teardrop helps control. Keep effects minimal so every note of the line speaks.\n\nIf you try the thumb, expect a smooth, warm attack, and build your octave lines from downstrokes.",cites:["b12","b8","b9"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"bebop-archtop",name:"Clean line tone",kind:"electric",guitar:"Archtop or semi-hollow (ES-175, L-5 or ES-335 type)",pickups:"Neck humbucker",strings:"Flatwound 12s or 13s",amp:"Clean combo (Fender Twin Reverb, Roland JC-120 or Polytone type)",settings:{gain:2,bass:4,mid:7,treble:4,presence:3,reverb:1},pedals:[],demo:"bebop-enclosure-251",tips:["Grant Green, on an ES-335, boosted the midrange and used little or no reverb, which keeps fast lines clear.","A small, thick pick such as a Jazz III or a heavy teardrop helps control at speed.","Use the neck pickup with the tone control partly down for a round attack.","Stay on the clean channel and let your picking hand make the accents."],cites:["b12"],note:"Push the mid knob and pull the reverb back to hear how much clearer a fast line gets."},{type:"tone",id:"bebop-thumb",name:"Thumb and octaves",kind:"electric",guitar:"Full hollowbody archtop (L-5 type)",pickups:"Neck humbucker",strings:"Flatwounds",amp:"Clean combo with headroom",settings:{gain:2,bass:6,mid:5,treble:3,presence:2,reverb:2},pedals:[],demo:"bebop-wes-octaves",tips:["Pluck with your thumb instead of a pick for a smooth, warm attack, as Wes Montgomery did.","Fret octaves with the first and little fingers and keep the string between them muted.","Use downstrokes for most eighth-note octave lines.","Wes Montgomery is associated with a Gibson L-5 archtop."],cites:["b8","b9","b12"],note:"Try a little more treble with the thumb than you would use with a pick, then compare the two."}]},{id:"form",title:"A rhythm changes chorus",body:"The song map is a full rhythm changes chorus in Bb: two A sections, the dominant bridge and a last A, then a solo chorus over the same 32 bars. The A sections are simplified to one chord per bar, so the I–vi–ii–V cycle that really moves twice as fast is spread over four bars.\n\nDrop the bridge etude from the lines step in where the map reaches D7. For your own head, write an A melody in eighth notes with room for a breath every two bars, and let the bridge answer it with arpeggios through the dominant chain.",cites:["b4"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Rhythm changes in Bb",sections:[{type:"intro",name:"Intro turnaround",bars:4,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7",groove:"bebop-head-hat",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A1 (head)",bars:8,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 V7",groove:"bebop-head-hat",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A2 (head)",bars:8,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 -",groove:"bebop-head-hat",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"bridge",name:"B (bridge)",bars:8,chords:"III7 - VI7 - II7 - V7 -",groove:"bebop-ride",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",name:"A3 (head)",bars:8,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 -",groove:"bebop-head-hat",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"solo",name:"Solo chorus (AABA)",bars:32,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 V7 I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 - III7 - VI7 - II7 - V7 - I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 -",groove:"bebop-ride",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"outro",name:"Head out (last A)",bars:8,chords:"I6 VI7 ii7 V7 I7 IV6 I6 -",groove:"bebop-head-hat",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Head on the hi-hat, bridge and solos on the ride. Add more solo choruses by repeating the solo section.",cites:["b4","b11"]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Learn the three etudes at 120 bpm, then 160, then 200, and stop raising the tempo when the chord tones stop landing on the beat. Then write two lines of your own for each chord pair: one with an enclosure into the 3rd, one with a descending bebop scale.\n\nPlay a chorus of rhythm changes over the song map. Comp the A sections with shells, solo through the bridge with arpeggios and end with an octave phrase in Wes's style. Record it and check that every chord change has a target note you meant to play.",cites:["b5","b6","b4","b8"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Play the dominant bebop scale down from F, A, C and Eb with chord tones on the beats.","Enclose the 3rd of every chord in the rhythm changes bridge.","Swap F7 for B7 in a ii–V–I and land a line on D.","Play a blues chorus in octaves with all downstrokes.","Solo one full rhythm changes chorus at 160 bpm without losing the form."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"gypsy-jazz",family:"Jazz",name:"Gypsy jazz (jazz manouche)",tagline:"Acoustic swing from Django Reinhardt's Paris: la pompe rhythm, minor 6 chords and arpeggio-driven solos.",level:"Intermediate",era:"1934 to today",artists:["Django Reinhardt","Stéphane Grappelli","Biréli Lagrène","Stochelo Rosenberg","Angelo Debarre","Tchavolo Schmitt","Fapy Lafertin","Joscho Stephan"],listen:["Django Reinhardt & Stéphane Grappelli – Minor Swing","Django Reinhardt – Nuages","Quintette du Hot Club de France – Djangology","The Rosenberg Trio – Caravan (album)","Biréli Lagrène – Routes to Django (album)"],key:"A minor",bpm:180,meter:"4/4",techniques:["string-muting","tremolo-picking"],sources:[{id:"g1",title:"Gypsy jazz",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gypsy_jazz",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"g2",title:"Django Reinhardt (excerpt from Django: The Life and Music of a Gypsy Legend)",author:"Michael Dregni",site:"DjangoBooks.com",year:2004,url:"https://www.djangobooks.com/blog/django_reinhardt/",kind:"book"},{id:"g3",title:"Major Swing: Django Reinhardt, His Disciples, and Their Hot Brand of Acoustic Jazz Guitar",author:"Paul Mehling",site:"Acoustic Guitar",year:2019,url:"https://acousticguitar.com/major-swing-django-reinhardt-his-disciples-and-their-hot-brand-of-acoustic-jazz-guitar/",kind:"article"},{id:"g4",title:"Rhythm Rules: Gypsy Jazz Primer",author:"Shawn Persinger",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2014,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/rhythm-rules-gypsy-jazz-primer",kind:"lesson"},{id:"g5",title:"Django's Gypsy Jazz Secrets",author:"Michael Horowitz",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/djangos-gypsy-jazz-secrets",kind:"lesson"},{id:"g6",title:"Decoding Django's Gypsy Chords",author:"Doug Munro",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2019,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/acoustic-lessons/django-gypsy-jazz-chords",kind:"lesson"},{id:"g7",title:"Selmer guitar",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selmer_guitar",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"g8",title:"Rosenberg Trio",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosenberg_Trio",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"g9",title:"Gypsy Picking",author:"Michael Horowitz",site:"Guitar Solo Publications",url:"https://gspguitar.com/gypsy-picking.html",kind:"book"},{id:"g10",title:"Gypsy Jazz Guitar: Rhythm & Chord Progressions",site:"JazzGuitar.be",url:"https://www.jazzguitar.be/blog/gypsy-jazz-chords/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"g11",title:"Quest for Volume",author:"Michael Dregni",site:"Vintage Guitar",year:2018,url:"https://www.vintageguitar.com/35031/quest-for-volume/",kind:"article"},{id:"g12",title:"What to Look for When Buying a Gypsy-Jazz Guitar, from Tone to Playability",author:"Adam Perlmutter",site:"Acoustic Guitar",year:2017,url:"https://acousticguitar.com/what-to-look-for-when-buying-a-gypsy-jazz-guitar-from-tone-to-playability/",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"Gypsy Picking",kind:"book",author:"Michael Horowitz",url:"https://gspguitar.com/gypsy-picking.html",note:"The standard method on rest-stroke picking, with notated pick strokes, common phrases and a minor blues solo."},{title:"La Pompe: The Ultimate Course for Gypsy Jazz Accompaniment",kind:"course",author:"Yaakov Hoter",url:"https://djangobooks.com/Item/yaakov-hoter-la-pompe",note:"Step-by-step lessons on la pompe, chord shapes, turnarounds and play-along tracks."},{title:"Gypsy Jazz Guitar Guidebook",kind:"course",author:"Reinier Voet",url:"https://truefire.com/c1029",note:"Video lessons on la pompe, minor 6 and diminished sounds, and soloing around chord tones."},{title:"Django Reinhardt & Stéphane Grappelli – The Quintessential: 25 Classics, 1934–1940",kind:"album",note:"The Quintette's prewar recordings, where the whole style is laid out."},{title:"The Rosenberg Trio – Caravan",kind:"album",note:"Modern Dutch gypsy jazz with tight la pompe and clean, fast lead lines."},{title:"Biréli Lagrène – Routes to Django",kind:"album",note:"Lagrène's first album, recorded as a teenager, steeped in Reinhardt's vocabulary."},{title:"Django: The Life and Music of a Gypsy Legend",kind:"book",author:"Michael Dregni",url:"https://www.djangobooks.com/Item/Django_bio_dregni_paper",note:"A biography built on interviews with Reinhardt's family, friends and bandmates, for the story behind the music."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"Django and the Hot Club",body:"Gypsy jazz grew from the music of Django Reinhardt and Stéphane Grappelli in 1930s Paris, and it remains closely tied to the Manouche Romani community. Their **Quintette du Hot Club de France** formed in late 1934 and was made almost entirely of strings: Reinhardt's lead guitar, two rhythm guitars, violin and double bass, with no drummer.\n\nIn 1928 a fire in Reinhardt's caravan badly burned his left hand and left his two smallest fingers twisted and limited in use. He relearned the guitar around the injury, using the damaged fingers mostly for chords, and built a style from three-note chords and arpeggios played two notes per string. Grappelli traced some of Reinhardt's new chord progressions to that grip.\n\nThe style faded after the swing era, came back from the 1970s and is still gaining players. Biréli Lagrène, Stochelo Rosenberg and Angelo Debarre carried it forward, and Reinhardt's tunes remain the core of the repertoire.",cites:["g1","g2","g3"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"The Festival Django Reinhardt at Samois-sur-Seine has run since 1968, and camps such as Django in June keep teaching the style.",cites:["g1"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Minor 6, 6/9 and diminished",body:"Gypsy jazz harmony avoids plain triads. Major chords usually become major 6, major 7 or 6/9 chords, and diminished chords appear often, including half-diminished chords leading into a dominant in minor keys.\n\n**Minor 6** chords take the place of minor 7 chords and give the music its dark, slightly diminished color. Small three- and four-note shapes keep the rhythm tight, and Reinhardt's injury pushed him toward exactly these compact voicings.\n\nOne minor 6 grip does a lot of work. Premier Guitar's gypsy jazz primer calls it a magic chord, because the same Am6 shape can also be heard as a D7 without its root and as several diminished chords. Learn it on the 6th string, then on the 5th, and you have most of a minor tune covered.",cites:["g1","g6","g4"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Minor swing-style changes",chords:"i6 - iv6 - V9 - i6 -",note:"Am6, Dm6, E9, Am6, two bars each: a minor i–iv–V loop of the kind heard in Minor Swing.",cites:["g4","g6"]},{type:"progression",name:"Minor ii–V–i",chords:"iiø7 V7b9 i6 -",note:"Bø7, E7b9, Am6: the half-diminished-to-dominant move in a minor key.",cites:["g1"]},{type:"progression",name:"Major I–VI–ii–V in G",key:"G major",chords:"I6/9 VI7 ii6 V7",note:"G6/9, E7, Am6, D7. Am6 stands where a bebop player might use Am7, and it shares notes with the D7 that follows.",cites:["g6","g4"]},{type:"shapes",title:"Gypsy jazz grips",shapes:[{name:"Am6",frets:"5 x 4 5 5 x"},{name:"Dm6",frets:"x 5 x 4 6 5"},{name:"E9",frets:"x 7 6 7 7 x"},{name:"Bø7",frets:"x 2 3 2 3 x"},{name:"G6/9",frets:"x x 5 4 5 5"}],note:"Four notes or fewer, muting the strings in between. The Am6 and Dm6 shapes stack the same chord tones (root, 6th, 3rd, 5th) from different root strings.",cites:["g6"]}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"La pompe",body:"**La pompe** (the pump) is the rhythm guitar part of gypsy jazz, and it does the job a drummer would do. The basic version is four short downstrokes per bar with a slight push on beats 2 and 4. Many players add a very quick upstroke just before beats 1 and 3.\n\nThe fretting hand makes the chop. Let the chord sound on each downstroke, then relax the pressure so the strings mute: that muting gives la pompe its drum-like sound. There are regional flavors, including Parisian, German and Dutch versions, so listen to different players. Teachers say la pompe takes daily practice to stay steady at speed.\n\nThe groove below sketches la pompe for the drum machine: a soft low hit on 1 and 3, a sharper hit on 2 and 4, and a ghost just before 1 and 3. Real gypsy jazz bands usually leave the drums out, so treat it as a click that swings.",cites:["g3","g4","g5","g10","g1"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",name:"La pompe pulse",role:"Verse",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{sn:"x..X.gx..X.g",hh:"...x.....x..",kk:"g.....g....."}},note:"Brush-style snare in the la pompe rhythm: soft on 1 and 3, accented on 2 and 4, with a ghost before 1 and 3 where the guitar's upstroke falls.",cites:["g4"]},{type:"tab",id:"gypsy-jazz-la-pompe",title:"La pompe on Am6, Dm6, E9",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:6,tone:"gypsy-jazz-rhythm",notes:[[0,6,5,2,"D"],[0,4,4,2,"D"],[0,3,5,2,"D"],[0,2,5,2,"D"],[6,6,5,1,"D,acc"],[6,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[6,3,5,1,"D,acc"],[6,2,5,1,"D,acc"],[11,3,5,1,"U,x"],[11,2,5,1,"U,x"],[12,6,5,2,"D"],[12,4,4,2,"D"],[12,3,5,2,"D"],[12,2,5,2,"D"],[18,6,5,1,"D,acc"],[18,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[18,3,5,1,"D,acc"],[18,2,5,1,"D,acc"],[23,3,5,1,"U,x"],[23,2,5,1,"U,x"],[24,5,5,2,"D"],[24,3,4,2,"D"],[24,2,6,2,"D"],[24,1,5,2,"D"],[30,5,5,1,"D,acc"],[30,3,4,1,"D,acc"],[30,2,6,1,"D,acc"],[30,1,5,1,"D,acc"],[35,2,6,1,"U,x"],[35,1,5,1,"U,x"],[36,5,5,2,"D"],[36,3,4,2,"D"],[36,2,6,2,"D"],[36,1,5,2,"D"],[42,5,5,1,"D,acc"],[42,3,4,1,"D,acc"],[42,2,6,1,"D,acc"],[42,1,5,1,"D,acc"],[47,2,6,1,"U,x"],[47,1,5,1,"U,x"],[48,5,7,2,"D"],[48,4,6,2,"D"],[48,3,7,2,"D"],[48,2,7,2,"D"],[54,5,7,1,"D,acc"],[54,4,6,1,"D,acc"],[54,3,7,1,"D,acc"],[54,2,7,1,"D,acc"],[59,3,7,1,"U,x"],[59,2,7,1,"U,x"],[60,5,7,2,"D"],[60,4,6,2,"D"],[60,3,7,2,"D"],[60,2,7,2,"D"],[66,5,7,1,"D,acc"],[66,4,6,1,"D,acc"],[66,3,7,1,"D,acc"],[66,2,7,1,"D,acc"],[71,3,7,1,"U,x"],[71,2,7,1,"U,x"],[72,6,5,2,"D"],[72,4,4,2,"D"],[72,3,5,2,"D"],[72,2,5,2,"D"],[78,6,5,1,"D,acc"],[78,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[78,3,5,1,"D,acc"],[78,2,5,1,"D,acc"],[83,3,5,1,"U,x"],[83,2,5,1,"U,x"],[84,6,5,2,"D"],[84,4,4,2,"D"],[84,3,5,2,"D"],[84,2,5,2,"D"],[90,6,5,1,"D,acc"],[90,4,4,1,"D,acc"],[90,3,5,1,"D,acc"],[90,2,5,1,"D,acc"],[95,3,5,1,"U,x"],[95,2,5,1,"U,x"]],note:"Four strummed downstrokes per bar, 2 and 4 choked short and pushed, with a muted upstroke flicked just before beats 1 and 3.",cites:["g4","g5"]},{type:"technique",id:"string-muting",note:"In la pompe the fretting hand lifts off right after each stroke, so the strings stop and the strum turns into a chop."}]},{id:"lines",title:"Arpeggios, diminished runs and rest strokes",body:"Gypsy jazz solos lean on ornamented **arpeggios** laid out diagonally across the neck, fast chromatic runs and diminished runs. Many of those patterns come from Reinhardt's two-finger technique, two stopped notes per string. Glissandos played with tremolo picking are another signature effect.\n\nOver minor 6 chords players reach for Dorian sounds. Over dominants they use Mixolydian, the bebop scale or harmonic minor, and a diminished arpeggio built a half step above the dominant's root targets its b9.\n\nThe picking hand uses **rest strokes**, a flatpick version of the classical apoyando. The weight of the hand drives the pick through the string until it comes to rest on the next one, which gives volume and tone with little tension. Michael Horowitz's book on the technique notates pick strokes for common phrases, based on study of Gypsy players and film of Reinhardt.",cites:["g1","g5","g9"],blocks:[{type:"scale",root:"A",scale:"harmMin",view:"box",note:"A harmonic minor: the G# gives E7 its leading tone, and the F supplies the b9.",cites:["g5"]},{type:"tab",id:"gypsy-jazz-minor-arps",title:"Am6 arpeggio and a diminished run over E7",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:3,tone:"gypsy-jazz-lead",notes:[[0,6,5,1,"D"],[1,6,8,1,"U"],[2,5,7,1,"D"],[3,5,9,1,"U"],[4,4,7,1,"D"],[5,4,10,1,"U"],[6,3,9,1,"D"],[7,3,11,1,"U"],[8,2,10,1,"D"],[9,2,13,3,"U,v"],[12,1,10,1],[13,1,7,1],[14,2,9,1],[15,2,6,1],[16,3,7,1],[17,3,4,1],[18,4,6,1],[19,3,4,1],[20,3,7,1],[21,2,6,3],[24,2,5,3,"v"],[27,3,5,3],[30,4,7,6,"v"]],note:"Bar 1 climbs an Am6 arpeggio two notes per string in triplets. Over E7 an F diminished arpeggio (F, Ab, B, D) supplies the b9, and F falls to E on the Am6.",cites:["g1","g5"]},{type:"tab",id:"gypsy-jazz-major-run",title:"G6 arpeggio and chromatic run",instr:"guitar",bpm:160,grid:3,tone:"gypsy-jazz-lead",notes:[[0,5,2,1,"D"],[1,5,5,1,"U"],[2,4,2,1,"D"],[3,4,5,1,"U"],[4,3,4,1,"D"],[5,3,7,1,"U"],[6,2,5,1,"D"],[7,2,8,1,"U"],[8,1,7,1,"D"],[9,1,10,3,"U"],[12,1,12,1,"D"],[13,1,11,1,"U"],[14,1,10,1,"D"],[15,1,9,1,"U"],[16,1,8,1,"D"],[17,1,7,1,"U"],[18,1,6,1,"D"],[19,1,5,1,"U"],[20,2,9,1,"D"],[21,2,8,3,"U,v"]],note:"A G6 arpeggio (B, D, E, G) in minor thirds, two notes per string, then a chromatic triplet run from E down to G.",cites:["g1","g5"]},{type:"technique",id:"tremolo-picking",note:"Tremolo picking on a gliss or a long note is one of the style's effects. Keep the wrist loose and the strokes small.",cites:["g1"]}]},{id:"tone",title:"Selmer-style projection",body:"The sound starts with the guitar. From 1932 Selmer built guitars to Mario Maccaferri's design: a gently arched top, a wide floating bridge, a tailpiece and, on the first models, a large D-shaped soundhole (grande bouche) with a resonating box hidden inside. After Maccaferri left, Selmer revised the design with a small oval soundhole (petite bouche), 14 frets clear of the body and a long 670 mm scale. Reinhardt moved to the oval-hole model in 1936 or 1937.\n\nThe small oval hole gives a more focused, directional sound that suits a soloist. In the Quintette, oval-hole guitars were typically used for lead and D-hole guitars for rhythm. The Rosenberg Trio keeps that split: Nous'che plays a D-hole guitar and Stochelo an oval-hole.\n\nPlayers commonly use silver-plated strings such as Savarez Argentine and a thick pick, sometimes up to 6 mm. The rest stroke adds volume and tone from the picking hand.",cites:["g11","g12","g7","g8","g5"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"gypsy-jazz-lead",name:"Oval-hole lead",kind:"acoustic",guitar:"Selmer-Maccaferri style, oval hole (petite bouche)",pickups:"None (acoustic, or a microphone)",strings:"Savarez Argentine",amp:"Unamplified",settings:{gain:0,bass:4,mid:6,treble:7,reverb:2},pedals:[],demo:"gypsy-jazz-minor-arps",tips:["The oval-hole Modèle Jazz has 14 frets clear of the body and a 670 mm scale, and its small soundhole gives a focused, directional sound for lead.","Savarez Argentine strings, silver-plated copper on a steel core, are the common choice.","Use a thick pick. Specialist gypsy jazz picks run up to 6 mm.","Let the pick come to rest on the next string after each downstroke: the rest stroke gives volume and tone with little tension."],cites:["g5","g11","g12"],note:"Most of this sound comes from the guitar and the picking hand, so keep the controls flat and listen to the attack."},{type:"tone",id:"gypsy-jazz-rhythm",name:"D-hole rhythm",kind:"acoustic",guitar:"Selmer-Maccaferri style, D-hole (grande bouche)",pickups:"None (acoustic)",strings:"Savarez Argentine",amp:"Unamplified",settings:{gain:0,bass:6,mid:5,treble:5,reverb:1},pedals:[],demo:"gypsy-jazz-la-pompe",tips:["In the Quintette, D-hole guitars were typically used for rhythm and oval-hole guitars for lead.","The Rosenberg Trio follows the same split: Nous'che on a D-hole guitar, Stochelo on an oval-hole.","Relax the fretting hand right after each downstroke so the strings mute between strokes.","Keep the downstrokes short and push beats 2 and 4 slightly."],cites:["g4","g5","g7","g8","g12"],note:"A rhythm guitar should sound short and woody. If the chords ring, release sooner."}]},{id:"form",title:"Tune form and the jam",body:"Reinhardt's compositions, such as 'Minor Swing' and 'Nuages', are standards of the style, and festivals and camps keep them in circulation. Minor 6 harmony and la pompe run through much of the repertoire.\n\nThe song map gives your tune a 32-bar minor AABA form in A minor. The A sections sit on i, iv and V in two-bar blocks, which leaves room for a melody built from Am6 and Dm6 arpeggios. The bridge moves to the iv chord, back to i, then through B7 to E7, a short dominant chain that pulls into the last A.\n\nSolos follow the same 32 bars with la pompe under every chorus. Start the tune with a four-bar E9 vamp so the band can lock in before the melody.",cites:["g3","g1","g6"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"32-bar minor swing in A minor",sections:[{type:"intro",name:"E9 vamp",bars:4,chords:"V9 - - -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A1 (head)",bars:8,chords:"i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A2 (head)",bars:8,chords:"i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"bridge",name:"B (bridge)",bars:8,chords:"iv6 - i6 - II7 - V7 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A3 (head)",bars:8,chords:"i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"solo",name:"Solo chorus (AABA)",bars:32,chords:"i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 - i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 - iv6 - i6 - II7 - V7 - i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:""},{type:"outro",name:"Head out (last A)",bars:8,chords:"i6 - iv6 - V7 - i6 -",groove:"gypsy-jazz-pompe",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Am6, Dm6, E7 in the A sections; Dm6, Am6, B7, E7 in the bridge.",cites:["g6"]}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Start with the rhythm. Play la pompe through the song map until the chops stay short and even at 120 bpm, then at 160. Record it, because this is the track you will solo over.\n\nWrite an A melody from Am6 and Dm6 arpeggios, two notes per string, and resolve each phrase to a chord tone. Over every E7, try the F diminished arpeggio and let its F fall to E.\n\nPick the whole head with rest strokes, and save one tremolo-picked gliss for the end of your solo. Trade choruses with a friend: one keeps la pompe going while the other solos.",cites:["g4","g5","g1"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Play la pompe on Am6, Dm6 and E9 at 120 bpm, then 160, with short, even chops.","Swap every minor 7 for a minor 6 in one tune you already know.","Play the Am6 arpeggio two notes per string with index and middle fingers only.","Play an F diminished arpeggio over every E7 in your solo and resolve F to E.","Pick a whole chorus with rest strokes and listen for volume without tension.","Trade choruses with a friend: one plays la pompe while the other solos."]}]}]});